WEBVTT

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:07.250
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic from the CECS Department at Cal State Long Beach. So this is one very, very

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:11.650
theoretically intensive set of videos.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:15.970
We want to make

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:26.850
a memory element, something that stores one bit, that is more robust than SR latch.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:34.850
And the gates have been getting to be smaller and cheaper as we were, you know,

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:40.730
evolving, the technology was evolving. So therefore people came up with something

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:54.100
that is called the D latch. Where S was set and R was reset, D is for data.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:01:16.260
So D latch also stores one bit and it is way more robust than SR latch. So let's

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:33.850
look into inputs and outputs first. So D latch has two inputs. One is going to be

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:48.280
input D where it is going to be the value of data bits to be stored and the

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:53.960
the other one is going to be E, enable signal.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.120
And we have already met with enable signal

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:02.160
in context of different combinational circuits.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:08.880
We have one output and this one output is going to be Q

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:14.160
and Q is, well actually let's do at least D capital

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.400
to be consistent with everything.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:22.400
So Q is an output and Q is value of the stored bit.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:41.300
So we are actually going to use SR latch

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.760
in order to implement the latch.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:50.420
Going to go and first make one instance of SR latch,

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:09.680
decrease cross the outputs.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.720
So this is where our S used to be.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.800
This is where our R used to be.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:16.720
This is going to be internal.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:22.720
And output Q is here.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:25.780
To make this robust,

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.920
we are going to connect it to two end gates.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:40.580
And I'm going to make this look as nice as possible.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:48.270
and what we are going to do is we are going to connect

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:53.270
enable signal to both of these end gates

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:57.270
now we are going to have the input

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:01.270
that is going to be connected to the top end gate

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:05.270
and then we are going to have a funny thing which is going to be

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:15.540
the knob connected to the bottom end gate

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:30.720
If we want to be more precise here and kind of outline what is inside and what is outside,

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:38.160
we can sometimes have this Q prim as an output, so I'm going to make it this way.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:49.660
But if you look into our D latch, the boundary for what is inside and what is outside is

00:04:49.660 --> 00:05:00.030
going to be here. So everything that is inside is the latch. Now how does the latch work?

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:07.650
I'm going to go and reduce the size of it so that we can have the table for SR latch

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:21.330
also included here. Ok, we have E as in Enable, maybe a little bit smaller, E as in Enable,

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:40.190
we have D and we have Q as an output. Okay, if E is equal to 0, 0 is here in this point

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:48.030
where 0 is input to both AND gates. When we have 0 as input to both AND gates, both AND

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:58.190
gates will have 0 as an output. Now let's look at the previous table. What does 0, 0

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:18.830
as an output of SR latch provide on the output of SR latch? It keeps previous and therefore

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:29.500
it really doesn't matter what is on the input for this. Now if E is equal to 1, we

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:33.000
have two possible options for D.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:36.280
D can be zero and D can be one.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:46.100
So E is equal to one.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:52.700
If E is equal to one and D is equal to zero,

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:58.380
this input here is going to be zero.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:00.460
This one input is going to be one.

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:12.460
So, therefore, for SAT, for S here, this is going to be 0, so 0 and 1 is 0, but for the

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:18.980
bottom one, the R input of SR latch, we are going to have 1, 1, so we are going to have

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:31.560
S equal to 0, R equals to 1. We look at the table and that is reset. Our Q becomes 0,

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:43.830
So that was the reset that we utilized from SRLatch.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:58.060
If we have DLatch equal to D input, sorry, on DLatch being equal to 1, and E is still

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:09.160
equal to 0. So d equals to 1 is going to have 1 here, is going to have 0 here. 1

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:16.060
and 1 on the top end gate is giving us 1. 1 and 0 on the bottom gate is giving

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:25.060
us 0. That is s equal to 1, r equals to 0, s equal to 1, r equal to 0. Set the

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:37.850
latch q becomes 1. If you see these two outputs they are literally copy of what

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:54.760
is on the input so this means when e is equal to 1 we store the value on the d

00:08:54.760 --> 00:09:07.960
input and that is very very useful thing to do. Now we are going to go even one

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:18.950
step further with this. We are going to work with D flip-flop in such a way to

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:28.410
use clock to synchronize and to make the moment when bit is stored is going to be

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:58.200
determined by clock. So clock pulse or clock signal. What we are going to have

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:04.040
we are going to have a clock and it really doesn't matter what is the beauty

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:11.520
cycle of this clock. And then we are going to connect this clock to enable

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:27.540
signal. What is going to happen here? If we connect this to the enable signal

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:48.180
that is. So during this time we will have case where e is equal to 1. So during e

00:10:48.180 --> 00:11:12.170
equals to 1 we will take the value on the input of e and store it

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:21.040
to our D latch. Now, yellow is not the

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:23.680
greatest color ever for this thing but

